---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CarrierPanel from '../components/CarrierPanel.astro';
import HeroSnippet from '../components/HeroSnippet.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { MEASURED_ON, libsignalReadme } from '../lib/comparison.mjs';
import { startupTier } from '../data/pricing.mjs';

const differentiators = [
  {
    title: 'Pure TypeScript, everywhere your app runs',
    /* "No native modules, no prebuild step, no ejecting" was true of the crypto
     * and false of the storage this site's own /product page documents: the
     * encrypted Expo store is expo-sqlite with SQLCipher, which Expo Go does
     * not carry. The limit belongs in the same breath as the capability.
     *
     * Appending the limit was not enough: "no prebuild step" and "needs a
     * development build" stayed in one sentence, and two readers called it a
     * contradiction, because a development build is native project generation
     * however it is spelled. The claim is now scoped the way the approved
     * proof point in docs/messaging.md §5 scopes it — to the *protocol code*,
     * which really is pure TypeScript — rather than to the package as a whole.
     * /product keeps the full reconciliation, where there is room to explain
     * that `npx expo prebuild` is not a command the reader types. */
    body: 'Expo, React Native, browsers, and Node from one package. The protocol code is pure TypeScript with no native crypto module to link — the encrypted Expo store uses SQLCipher, which needs a development build rather than Expo Go.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Post-quantum from the first handshake',
    body: 'PQXDH with ML-KEM establishes the session; the Double Ratchet carries it forward. Not a future roadmap item.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Your backend stays yours',
    body: 'Storage, relay, and encrypted-object adapters are composed by your application. We do not host anything, and nothing phones home.',
  },
  {
    /* The clause here used to be "the work teams usually discover three
       months in". Two fresh readers flagged it independently and both were
       right: "three months" is a statistic, nobody measured it, and there are
       no teams to have measured it on. One wrote "unsourced vendor line —
       which teams? I discount it entirely", the other "a made-up number in a
       page that is otherwise scrupulous about sourcing". That is the exact
       trade the rest of the page refuses — borrowing the authority of a
       measurement without doing one — and it was buying a rhetorical beat.
       The replacement says the same thing as a fact about scope, which any
       reader can check against their own prototype. */
    title: 'The hard parts are included',
    body: 'Multi-device, groups, sealed sender, safety numbers, encrypted attachments and files — the parts a working one-to-one prototype still leaves to build.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Every claim is inspectable',
    body: 'The implementation, the protocol profile, the threat model, and the exact list of metadata the relay still sees are all published.',
  },
  {
    /* This cell said "at a published price" and showed no price, for the whole
     * loop. A fresh reader took the word "published" literally and was right
     * to: "it promises a number that is not on the page and not linked", and
     * they listed it as one of two go/no-go inputs they could not get. The
     * number existed the entire time — /pricing has carried $5,000 since
     * before this loop started, and the header links to it. The page was
     * being coy about the one fact a solo developer decides on.
     *
     * The price is read from the same module /pricing renders, so the two
     * surfaces cannot disagree. Naming the entry tier only, because "starts
     * at" is the honest shape of a three-tier table and the link carries the
     * rest. */
    id: 'agpl-or-commercial',
    title: 'AGPL, or a commercial license',
    body: `The complete SDK is free under AGPL-3.0 — every feature, nothing time-limited. Closed-source products buy annual production rights, from ${startupTier.price} ${startupTier.period} for one product.`,
    link: { href: '/pricing', label: 'See the tiers' },
  },
  /*
   * The band is titled "What you get, and what it costs you to find out" and
   * for eight rounds it listed only the first half. Three fresh readers in two
   * consecutive waves arrived at the missing half unaided and made it their
   * largest gap: one asked what happens to dispute resolution, fraud review
   * and trust-and-safety; one wrote that support messages under E2EE cannot be
   * produced under subpoena, cannot be supervised, and cannot have DLP run
   * over them, and that "the page never names the tradeoff — not to solve it,
   * not even to acknowledge it exists." Their shared conclusion was not that
   * the product is wrong. It is that a page which omits the one cost every
   * evaluator finds in the first meeting has not met an evaluator yet, and
   * that reading is more expensive than the cost itself.
   *
   * It is stated as a consequence of what the relay holds, because that is the
   * part this page can prove: the recorded row two bands down is ciphertext,
   * and everything in this cell follows from that and nothing else. No
   * regulation is named — docs/messaging.md bans "required for HIPAA / GDPR /
   * SOC 2" outright, and the honest form of this cost is the capability a team
   * loses, not a rule someone must obey.
   *
   * The last sentence sends the wrong reader away on purpose. The brief
   * optimises for qualified starts of a ten-minute quickstart, and a team that
   * needs server-side moderation will discover this either way — the only
   * variable is whether they discover it here or after the ten minutes, and
   * only one of those two outcomes is repairable.
   *
   * It said "if your product needs any of that" for one round, and that was
   * too wide. A fresh reader building a marketplace pointed out that
   * client-side reporting — the user's own device attaches the plaintext to a
   * report — is a solved pattern, so the page was disqualifying products it
   * actually fits, in the last sentence they would read before closing the
   * tab. The condition is server-side capability, not the capability itself,
   * and the sentence now says so. It stops there deliberately: the SDK ships
   * no reporting API, and describing the client-side pattern as though it
   * were one would trade an over-disqualification for an invented feature.
   */
  {
    id: 'what-you-give-up',
    title: 'What you give up',
    body: 'Your relay holds ciphertext, so nothing on your backend can search message contents, moderate them, or produce them for a legal request. The SDK ships encrypted device-to-device transfer, but restoring history to a user who has lost every device is a backup you design. If any of that has to happen on your server, encrypt in transit and at rest instead — this is the wrong tool, and it is cheaper to know now.',
  },
];

/*
 * The runtime question is the first one a visitor has, and one quickstart per
 * runtime makes "does it run where I run" a click instead of a search. It sits
 * under the differentiator that raises it rather than in the hero, where three
 * more links were competing with the one action the hero is for.
 */
const runtimes = [
  { href: 'https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/expo', label: 'Expo' },
  { href: 'https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/browser', label: 'Browser' },
  { href: 'https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/node', label: 'Node' },
];

/*
 * Everything a reader can check, in the band that exists to be checked. The
 * security model and the review pack used to sit in a third row of links in
 * the hero, where they read as afterthoughts to the CTA rather than as the
 * page's answer to "prove it".
 */
const trustLinks = [
  { href: '/security', label: 'What the relay can and cannot see' },
  { href: '/evaluate', label: 'The security review pack' },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main/docs/SECURITY.md',
    label: 'The security model and threat model',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md',
    label: 'Architecture and protocol profile',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js',
    label: 'View the SDK on GitHub',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="OpenE2EE"
  description="Add end-to-end encrypted messaging to a TypeScript app. The Signal Protocol in one npm package for Expo, React Native, browsers, and Node — your relay never needs message plaintext or device private keys."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>
          <span class="keep-together">End-to-end</span> encrypted messaging for your TypeScript app.
        </h1>
        {/* "It is not a hosted chat service" is four words of comparison in
            the one block that was already above the fold everywhere.

            A mobile critic measured the first paint and found the ratio: every
            pixel of disclosed risk was above the 664 line and none of the
            disclosed differentiation was — the libsignal argument begins 19 px
            under it. Their summary was that the page is honest in the wrong
            order, because the reasoning a phone shows is "this is exactly my
            stack, my users' stored data may break, click here", with no
            evidence and no comparison in between. Six critics have now listed
            what they would otherwise reach for and every list is topped by the
            same category, a hosted chat service. Against that one the page
            already had its answer — the ownership clause and "nothing phones
            home" are that answer — but stated as a privacy fact, so no reader
            heard it as a comparison. It is the same sentence with the other
            side of it named.

            No vendor is named and none is characterised: the claim is about
            this product, and /pricing states the same thing in its own words
            ("You run your own infrastructure"). A comparison the page can only
            make by describing someone else's product is one it should not make
            here.

            It was budgeted as costing a line at 390 — the fold holds four
            answers and there are five, so every addition was expected to come
            out of the secondary button. Measured, it costs nothing there. The
            lead's sixth line held one word, `home.`, and 33 characters
            redistributed into the slack above it: the CTA, its terms and the
            GitHub button are on the same pixels as before, 541, 593 and 628.
            The 31 px it does cost lands at 1280 and 1440, where the fold is
            250 px further down and nothing reaches it.

            Which is worth recording as the reason, not as luck. Ragged-right
            body copy carries a line of slack most of the time, and the fold
            arithmetic from round 4 counts blocks rather than characters, so it
            prices a clause and a paragraph the same. It is right about
            paragraphs and wrong about clauses.

            "and more than TLS" is the second comparison, and it is here
            because the first one was aimed at the wrong opponent. Nine fresh
            readers have now written out the shortlist they hold before the
            page speaks, and a hosted chat service tops it — but it is never
            the whole list, and it is never the option they would actually
            take. Three of the last four named the same real default in the
            same words: ship it on TLS and encryption at rest, revisit E2EE
            later. One called it "my actual default", another "the real
            default and the hardest to displace". The page argued against
            hosted chat and against libsignal, which are the two alternatives
            it finds most flattering to argue against, and said nothing at all
            to the reader who is not going to encrypt anything.

            Naming TLS is not a comparison with a vendor, and it does not
            characterise anyone's product. TLS and at-rest encryption are the
            reader's own status quo, and the honest distinction between them
            and this is where the protection stops — which is a fact about
            where ciphertext sits, and the recorded relay row further down is
            the evidence for it.

            "carries them as ciphertext" and not "cannot read them": §3 of
            docs/messaging.md bans that paraphrase by name, and the paired
            honesty line under the button still says the relay sees metadata.
            The claim is about the envelope, which is the thing the page can
            show, and does.

            It read "carries them sealed" for one round, and all three fresh
            readers of that round stopped on the word. "sealed" is a term of
            art here — the feature band lists "sealed sender" two screens
            down, and the recorded relay row prints `senderUserId alice` in
            the clear. So the hero appeared to promise a specific feature that
            the page's own evidence then showed switched off. One reader
            called it "the one place I felt handled", which is the most
            expensive sentence a reader can write about this page.
            "ciphertext" is the word the recorded panel, the cost cell, and
            this line can all use to mean the same thing.

            Paid for by "nothing phones home", which is not lost — it is the
            second sentence of the "Your backend stays yours" cell, verbatim.
            A payload that appears twice on one page can be spent once. */}
        <p class="lead">
          The Signal Protocol as one npm package, for Expo, React Native, browsers, and Node. Not a
          hosted chat service, and more than TLS: no third party holds your users&rsquo; messages,
          and your own relay carries them as ciphertext. You own the device store and the relay; the
          SDK ships adapters for both.
        </p>
        {/* This line gates the offer, so it sits directly above the button
            rather than closing the column. A reader who reaches the button has
            not necessarily read down to a footnote, and the click happens
            here. It gates runtime scope as well as maturity: the lead sells
            four runtimes as peers, and the admission that two of their stores
            are experimental otherwise sits four folds below the button, so a
            browser or bare React Native developer could spend the whole ten
            minutes before meeting it. Its rule is the join between the
            argument and the offer, not a section break under both. */}
        {/* The licence sentence links because three fresh readers were told
            three times that they owe money and never once what it costs. One
            made it their largest gap on the whole page: the AGPL card says
            proprietary products buy production rights "at a published price",
            which asserts the price is public in the act of not showing it —
            the one claim on a page built around inspectable claims that they
            could not inspect. Pricing was a bare nav word thirteen screens
            below the sentence that created the debt. /pricing carries the
            numbers, so this is a missing link and not a missing decision. */}
        {/* The licence sentence is here because of a contradiction four fresh
            readers hit and none could resolve: the nav offers Pricing and
            Console while the lead says "nothing phones home" and the button
            says "no account". Every one of them read Pricing as a hosted
            service and concluded the page was hiding one. It sells a
            commercial licence — "Free under AGPL. Priced when you need it
            closed", and "You run your own infrastructure, you keep your own
            keys", both on /pricing — so the two statements were never in
            tension; the landing page simply never said which kind of thing
            Pricing was. One reader called it the only place where reading
            longer reduced their confidence.

            It belongs in this strip rather than the lead because this strip is
            the disqualifying-facts gate in front of the button, and a licence
            disqualifies more readers than an alpha version number does. */}
        {/* "; Expo and Node are not" is four words closing an inference five
            separate readers have now had to make for themselves.

            The sentence marks two of the four runtimes the lead sells as
            peers, and readers split two ways on it. One kind read "Browser and
            bare React Native stores are experimental" as *browsers are
            experimental*, so the page was harsher on itself than the facts
            are. The other kind reasoned correctly by elimination — Expo is the
            one runtime named in the lead and absent from the warning — and
            then said, in three separate reports, that they had constructed
            that answer rather than read it. An Expo founder put it plainly:
            the fold names Expo first, invites the question "can I actually use
            this", contains every word needed to answer it, and leaves the
            answer to the reader. They also could not tell whether "bare React
            Native" included them, having run `expo prebuild`.

            Naming the other side is the whole fix, and it is what the source
            says: `ADAPTERS.md` marks the web and react-native stores
            `(experimental)` and puts no marker on the Expo or Node ones. "Are
            not" rather than "are complete" or "are production-ready", because
            an absent marker is evidence for the absence of the marker and not
            for a capability grade — and the alpha clause in front of it
            already governs all four. */}
        <p class="maturity">
          0.1.x alpha — public APIs and persisted formats may change before 1.0. Browser and bare
          React Native stores are experimental; Expo and Node are not. Free under AGPL-3.0; <a
            href="/pricing">commercial licence for closed source</a
          >.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="cta-primary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
            <span class="oe-button">Build your first encrypted message</span>
            <span class="cta-sublabel">ten minutes · two clients · no account</span>
          </a>
          <a class="oe-button oe-button-secondary" href="https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js">
            <Icon name="github" />
            View the SDK on GitHub
          </a>
        </div>
        {/*
          These two paragraphs answer objections, and they now follow the offer
          instead of standing between the reader and it.

          A phone is why. 390×844 is the device height, not the viewport: an
          iPhone serves about 664 CSS px on first paint, and the button used to
          begin 91 px below that with the maturity rule landing on 661 — the
          last row of pixels a reader saw was a full-width line that means "this
          section has ended". Measured, the four blocks a first paint has room
          for are the headline, the lead, the alpha gate and the button; there
          is no ordering of six that fits five answers into 664 px, so the
          question was which answer arrives after a scroll rather than whether
          one does. It is now "why not the alternatives", which begins 13 px
          below the fold, instead of "what do I do next", which began 91 px
          below it.

          Nothing is lost at 390×844 either, where both still render.

          The libsignal sentence: three fresh readers answered every question
          the first viewport is for except this one, each giving the same
          reason — the page named no alternative, so "no native modules" was a
          contrast with nothing on the other side of it. Its own README is the
          strongest evidence available and it is one line long. The runtime
          clause repeats verbatim in the comparison band below, which is why
          the hero can afford to carry it and the fold can afford to lose it.
        */}
        {/* Twelve fresh readers have now written out the shortlist they hold
            before the page speaks, and every one of them tops it with the same
            option: ship it on TLS and encryption at rest, revisit E2EE later.
            The lead names that option — "and more than TLS" — and three
            readers in one wave independently returned the same verdict on it:
            "four words, no threat named", "an assertion, not an argument",
            "the entire argument against my number-one option". Naming an
            alternative is not arguing against it.

            The argument is in docs/positioning.md §5, where readable-data
            exposure is ranked the strongest adoption trigger of the eight, and
            its wording is deliberate: "end-to-end encryption can limit the
            readable content that an attacker gets from a breached relay." It
            says *can limit*, not prevents, and it is about a breached relay
            rather than about compliance — the same section opens by stating
            that almost no regulation requires E2EE at all. Reproduced close to
            verbatim here for that reason, hedge included.

            It sits above the libsignal sentence because the shortlists are
            ordered and libsignal is never first. It cannot go in the lead: at
            390 the real fold is 664 px and holds five blocks with no slack,
            and the only block that could yield is the alpha and licence
            disclosure, which the honesty contract requires and a test pins. */}
        <p class="stance">
          Why not just TLS? TLS protects a message on the wire and at-rest encryption protects the
          disk &mdash; in between, your server holds it readable. End-to-end encryption can limit
          the readable content a breached relay gives up.
        </p>
        <p class="stance">
          Why not libsignal? The reference implementation&rsquo;s own README says{' '}
          <a href={libsignalReadme.href}>&ldquo;use outside of Signal is unsupported&rdquo;</a>, and
          it does not run in Expo, React Native, or a browser.
        </p>
        {/* "we document exactly what" promised a document and pointed at
            nothing, in the one paragraph where a sceptical reader most wants
            to click through — a claim that cannot be checked at the moment it
            is being weighed. `/security` is where pricing, learn and product
            already send a reader for this same fact. */}
        <p class="honesty">
          The relay never needs message plaintext or device private keys. It still sees metadata —{' '}
          <a href="/security">we document exactly what</a>, and what sealed sender does and
          doesn&rsquo;t hide.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* The demo takes the side the proof panel used to hold. A reader
          deciding whether to spend ten minutes is asking what the API looks
          like, and this answers it without a click; the panel that answers
          "what does the relay actually hold" now sits with the diagram that
          raises the question. */}
      <div class="hero-demo">
        <HeroSnippet />
        <p class="muted">
          Send and receive, lifted from the quickstart as recorded.{' '}
          <a href="/product">See the whole file.</a>
        </p>
        {/*
          The disclosure was one clause at the end of the caption above, and it
          named Bob's client and the device registration. Four fresh readers
          sized up the excerpt; every one of them found something it did not
          name. `mockStore()` is a mock in the reader's own estimate — a real
          store, per device, per platform, and the thing whose "persisted
          formats may change before 1.0". `relay` is a server somebody has to
          run. Those are the two line items that decide whether this is a
          two-week or a two-month job, and they were the two the disclosure
          left out, which is worse than disclosing nothing: a reader who finds
          the omission themselves stops believing the parts you did admit.

          It is a list because a list can be checked against the code beside
          it, and a trailing clause cannot. It sits here because this is where
          the measured space was — two readers independently put a rectangle
          around the same emptiness under the caption, one calling it 630×137
          CSS px at 1280 and the other 569×238 at 1440, and both said the
          question the excerpt raises is asked exactly where nothing answers
          it. Every item is already on the page: two are in the code, one is in
          the lead, one was already in the caption.
        */}
        <div class="excerpt-drops">
          <p class="excerpt-drops-title">Not in this excerpt, and yours to supply</p>
          {/* The bullets name the shipped adapter as well as the obligation,
              because naming only the obligation created a contradiction.

              "The SDK ships adapters for both" is in the lead, 450 px to the
              left of "yours to supply", and a fresh reader put the two side by
              side and could not tell which was true: do I install an adapter
              and configure it, or do I stand up a network service before one
              real message moves? Their words — the difference between an
              afternoon and a sprint, and the only question that decides
              whether I try this today.

              Both sentences were true and the pair was not. The store and the
              relay are yours to supply; the code for them ships. `ADAPTERS.md`
              lists `expoStore`, `nodeStore`, `indexedDbStore` and
              `reactNativeStore`, and `convexRelay` alongside `mockRelay` — all
              exports of the installed package the build audit checks names
              against. So the answer is deploy, not write, and it took naming
              them to say so.

              The relay gets a definition because three fresh readers in one
              wave made the same complaint independently: it is the
              load-bearing noun of the whole pitch, it appears five times in
              the first viewport, and the page never says what it is. Each
              inferred "a server" and then could not size it. The description
              is the SDK's own — public keys, device lists, encrypted envelope
              delivery.

              What it deliberately does not say is what the relay can see. One
              reader proposed "it never sees message content or your keys",
              which is the absolute `design/DESIGN.md:403` forbids. The
              `.honesty` paragraph already carries the fixed formula and the
              metadata admission, one column away. A second, looser statement
              of the same boundary is how the loose one ends up quoted. */}
          <ul>
            <li>
              a real store in place of <code>mockStore()</code>, on each device — <code
                >expoStore()</code
              >, <code>nodeStore()</code> and <code>indexedDbStore()</code> ship
            </li>
            <li>
              the relay <code>mockRelay()</code> stands in for: the server that holds public keys
              and device lists and delivers the encrypted envelopes. <code>convexRelay()</code> ships
              one; you host it.
            </li>
            <li>registering both devices with it</li>
            <li>Bob&rsquo;s own client, which is Alice&rsquo;s four lines renamed</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>What you get, and what it costs you to find out</h2>
        <p class="measure">
          {/* The deck used to end "including the one that decides it against",
              which names a disqualifier and then withholds it. A reader whose
              product cannot live without server-side moderation had to read
              all seven cards to find the one that tells them to leave, and the
              whole point of putting it on the page is that they leave early
              and cheaply. A tease is the wrong shape for a warning. */}
          Seven things that decide whether protocol-level encryption is workable for your product.
          Read <a href="#what-you-give-up">What you give up</a> first &mdash; it rules this out for
          products with retention, moderation, or account-recovery duties, and it is cheaper to know
          now.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="rows">
        {differentiators.map((item) => (
          <li id={item.id}>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
            {/* Only the licence cell carries one. A row of links here would
                flatten the band into a nav list; one link, on the one cell
                whose answer lives on another page, reads as a continuation. */}
            {item.link && (
              <p class="row-link">
                <a href={item.link.href}>{item.link.label}</a>
              </p>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="platform-links">
        <span>Start where you are:</span>
        {runtimes.map((runtime) => <a href={runtime.href}>{runtime.label}</a>)}
      </p>
      {/* The three quickstarts used to sit here as equal peers, which is not
          what the shipped package says: ADAPTERS.md marks the browser and bare
          React Native stores experimental. A reader who learns that after
          following the link learns it too late.

          The Node clause tracks the shipped release, not the pinned one:
          alpha.9 completed `NodeSignalProtocolStore` — sender keys, device
          records and message records — so the sentence that named it short of
          groups and multi-device was true of the alpha.7 in node_modules and
          false of the alpha.9 the install command above resolves to. */}
      <p class="muted measure">
        The Expo and Node stores implement the storage interface in full. The browser and bare
        React Native stores are experimental.{' '}
        <a href="/product">What each store holds</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>One message, end to end</h2>
        {/* "ciphertext" and not "sealed", for the same reason the lead gives
            at length: "sealed sender" is a real feature of this SDK, and the
            recorded row four lines below this sentence prints `senderUserId`
            in the clear because the quickstart does not switch it on. This is
            the worse of the two places to be loose about the word — it is the
            caption standing directly over the evidence that would contradict
            it. The second clause was always honest; now the first one uses a
            word the panel underneath can be checked against. */}
        <p class="measure">
          Plaintext and private keys exist only on the devices at each end. Everything in between is
          ciphertext — and carries metadata your relay can still see.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* The signature diagram stood here for seven rounds and has been moved
          off this page. It is still the best drawing on the site and it still
          runs on /security and /product, where it is the only signature device
          on the screen — which is the design contract's rule and part of why
          it had to leave this one.

          The deciding test the brief sets is whether a fresh reader reaches
          the central boundary *faster* with the figure. An integration critic
          measured the opposite. The figure's whole vocabulary — readable
          device, seal, sealed envelope with metadata riding outside, relay,
          open, readable device — is the paragraph above it, drawn; its one
          genuine addition is that the boundary is crossed twice. The plate
          100 px below names the eight fields the relay actually held, with
          real values and a real byte count. Same boundary, with proof instead
          of assertion. A reader did not arrive sooner for the figure; they
          arrived sooner by scrolling past it, and on a phone that meant
          scrolling past ~770 px — more than one entire 664 px viewport of
          abstraction standing between them and the evidence.

          Three other things followed it out. Its content bars are the only
          thing distinguishing a readable device from an empty box, and they
          measure 1.93:1 light and 2.26:1 dark against the 3:1 that WCAG 1.4.11
          asks of a meaning-bearing graphic — in dark the two devices read as
          empty rectangles, which inverts the figure's own claim. The token is
          owned by @open-e2ee/design, so this page could not fix it, only stop
          depending on it. The contract's enforceable list also caps a screen
          at one signature device, naming "the mark, a manifest plate, and the
          signature diagram in one screen" as the failure; at a 937 px viewport
          all three were on screen together. And the relay is drawn as the
          OpenE2EE mark, so the one node the page insists you host looked like
          the vendor's logo, 60 px under the same logo in the header.

          Keeping it was the round-4 decision and it was provisional then — the
          note said to revisit if no critic reported the boundary landing
          faster with it. None ever did. */}
      <div id="what-the-relay-stores" class="carrier-proof">
        <h3>The same message, recorded</h3>
        <p class="measure">
          Not a mock-up: the plaintext as Bob&rsquo;s device decrypted it, and the row the relay
          held, captured by running the documented quickstart against the mock relay.
        </p>
